illustrated by your big pants,

00:56:40.600 --> 00:56:44.750
is we've kinda, in ways that maybe people

00:56:44.750 --> 00:56:46.730
that we went to high school with,

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:51.195
that didn't choose to be
YouTubers for a living,

00:56:51.195 --> 00:56:54.860
don't necessarily follow the trends.

00:56:54.860 --> 00:56:56.060
Now one of the things...

00:56:57.030 --> 00:56:58.300
We've talked about fashion before,

00:56:58.300 --> 00:57:00.470
so we were gonna do a podcast about that,

00:57:00.470 --> 00:57:01.940
but we're not.

00:57:01.940 --> 00:57:05.290
But one of the things I was
talking about with Jessie,

00:57:05.290 --> 00:57:10.290
I was like something about
now that I've got kids who...

00:57:12.790 --> 00:57:15.420
Like I've got a teenager in my house,

00:57:15.420 --> 00:57:17.040
and if I were to just say I'm gonna go to

00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:19.160
Urban Outfitters and I'm
just gonna buy everything

00:57:19.160 --> 00:57:21.860
on the rack at Urban
Outfitters and dress that way,

00:57:21.860 --> 00:57:25.589
now I'm dressing the same
way as my 15 year old kid.

00:57:25.589 --> 00:57:28.540
And at some point it kinda feels like

00:57:29.990 --> 00:57:31.950
I don't wanna do that anymore.

00:57:31.950 --> 00:57:33.687
And I told Jessie, I was like,

00:57:33.687 --> 00:57:36.827
"Is it because Locke is now a teenager

00:57:36.827 --> 00:57:40.157
"and then we're going into the same store

00:57:40.157 --> 00:57:41.827
"and we're looking at the same clothes,

00:57:41.827 --> 00:57:44.017
"and for the first time ever I'm just

00:57:44.017 --> 00:57:45.777
"going into a place like Urban Outfitters

00:57:45.777 --> 00:57:47.987
"and just thinking I don't know if there's

00:57:47.987 --> 00:57:49.287
"anything in here for me."

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:53.297
And she said, "Well, it
could be a part of it,

00:57:53.297 --> 00:57:56.927
"but I also think that
you've gotten to an age

00:57:56.927 --> 00:57:59.677
"where you kinda know
what looks good on you.

00:57:59.677 --> 00:58:03.807
"You kinda know what
shapes look good on you,

00:58:03.807 --> 00:58:05.570
"and you may not like the shapes."

00:58:05.570 --> 00:58:07.187
Like Shia LaBeouf said on Hot Ones.

00:58:07.187 --> 00:58:09.835
And that brought us back to our favorite

00:58:09.835 --> 00:58:12.020
Hot Ones episode ever of Shia LaBeouf

00:58:12.020 --> 00:58:13.780
talking about how the way he sees clothes

00:58:13.780 --> 00:58:15.659
is he just thinks about shapes,

00:58:15.659 --> 00:58:16.670
he doesn't think about colors or patterns.

00:58:16.670 --> 00:58:18.647
He thinks about shapes.

00:58:18.647 --> 00:58:21.447
And I was like I've never
connected more with something.

00:58:22.975 --> 00:58:24.040
I think about shapes, and it probably has

00:58:24.040 --> 00:58:26.863
something to do with just being a big man.

00:58:27.925 --> 00:58:31.499
But anyway, I found myself
resistant, like you know,

00:58:31.499 --> 00:58:34.020
and you've always been a little bit more,

00:58:34.020 --> 00:58:37.510
like if there's a fashion
trend that's gonna happen,

00:58:37.510 --> 00:58:40.715
you're gonna open that
door earlier than I am.

00:58:40.715 --> 00:58:43.318
And I've got reasons I won't go into

00:58:43.318 --> 00:58:47.480
that are boring about
why I think I delay that.

00:58:47.480 --> 00:58:51.899
But, this latest trend with the 90s

00:58:51.899 --> 00:58:54.837
and all the athletic
clothes and the boxy things

00:58:54.837 --> 00:58:59.400
and the block colors and
horizontal stripes and all this.

00:58:59.400 --> 00:59:01.463
I'm not on board for this one.

00:59:02.652 --> 00:59:06.799
And I feel like I'm kind of
taking an exit a little bit.

00:59:06.799 --> 00:59:10.160
Like I've been on this
interstate for a while

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:13.670
that's sorta like okay I'm kinda
conscious of fashion trends

00:59:13.670 --> 00:59:15.950
and I try to do something
that seems fashionable

00:59:15.950 --> 00:59:18.020
and I kinda dress like a young person,

00:59:18.020 --> 00:59:20.410
but I've gotta feel like I'm on the exit.

00:59:20.410 --> 00:59:24.040
And I'm like I'm 42, I think
I'm gonna have my own style

00:59:24.040 --> 00:59:25.580
and I'm not gonna be too worried about

00:59:25.580 --> 00:59:27.820
what the general style is anymore.

00:59:27.820 --> 00:59:29.740
And I'm just gonna wear
what I feel good in.

00:59:29.740 --> 00:59:31.640
You know what I'm saying?

00:59:31.640 --> 00:59:33.380
The decision I've made is more about

00:59:33.380 --> 00:59:37.270
I'm gonna get on a parallel highway

00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:38.560
that still is moving.

00:59:38.560 --> 00:59:43.470
I like trying new things,
but I don't wanna emulate,

00:59:43.470 --> 00:59:46.140
I don't wanna start looking like my son.

00:59:46.140 --> 00:59:48.260
I mean a lot of shirts
are getting a little

00:59:48.260 --> 00:59:51.550
too small for me, I end
up giving 'em to Lincoln.

00:59:51.550 --> 00:59:53.824
He likes maybe a third of 'em.

00:59:53.824 --> 00:59:55.420
He'll wear some of 'em.

00:59:55.420 --> 00:59:58.085
But yeah, that's waning.

00:59:58.085 --> 01:00:00.553
It's just a different aesthetic.

01:00:01.720 --> 01:00:04.663
But I like to have fun with an aesthetic.

01:00:05.880 --> 01:00:08.090
But I just want it to be my own thing.

01:00:08.090 --> 01:00:10.750
It's not just exiting and like parking.

01:00:10.750 --> 01:00:13.010
It's like well I'm just gonna,

01:00:13.010 --> 01:00:15.830
I'm gonna go full dad.

01:00:15.830 --> 01:00:17.228
I agree with that.

01:00:17.228 --> 01:00:18.061
Pop out to Gap.

01:00:18.061 --> 01:00:20.499
Well that's what I don't wanna do because,

01:00:20.499 --> 01:00:23.070
you got somebody like...

01:00:24.610 --> 01:00:26.300
I don't go on Facebook anymore,

01:00:26.300 --> 01:00:29.670
but in the same way
like personally going on

01:00:29.670 --> 01:00:33.190
and looking at old friends
photos and stuff like that.

01:00:33.190 --> 01:00:36.602
But I remember a decade ago
when I did such a thing,

01:00:36.602 --> 01:00:39.880
you'd go on there and so a decade ago

01:00:39.880 --> 01:00:43.370
I'm just over 30 years
old, and you go on there

01:00:43.370 --> 01:00:44.830
and you see somebody you
went to high school with

01:00:44.830 --> 01:00:48.500
and you're like man, that
dude has just, he's old.

01:00:48.500 --> 01:00:51.190
It isn't oh he aged.

01:00:51.190 --> 01:00:53.670
It might be that with
somebody who's like lost hair

01:00:53.670 --> 01:00:56.370
or their hair turned gray
or something like that.

01:00:56.370 --> 01:00:58.170
A lot of it is conformity.

01:00:58.170 --> 01:01:01.713
They made an aesthetic
decision to be like...

01:01:01.713 --> 01:01:04.470
This is my job, I'm a professional,

01:01:04.470 --> 01:01:08.770
and so I'm going to dress
and behave the same way

01:01:08.770 --> 01:01:11.550
that everyone else at
this place of work does.

01:01:11.550 --> 01:01:13.917
Up to age 65/70 years old, right?

01:01:13.917 --> 01:01:16.459
And so it's just like you
see this 30 year old person

01:01:16.459 --> 01:01:18.660
and you're like man, you
don't seem youthful anymore,

01:01:18.660 --> 01:01:21.737
you seem old, because you've conformed.

01:01:21.737 --> 01:01:24.110
Instead of there being a slow progression

01:01:24.110 --> 01:01:26.020
you just said I gotta get under this lane,

01:01:26.020 --> 01:01:27.800
I gotta get into professional lane,

01:01:27.800 --> 01:01:30.210
so now I got my polo
tucked into my khakis.

01:01:30.210 --> 01:01:31.043
Right.

01:01:31.043 --> 01:01:33.400
But we are in the art lane.

01:01:33.400 --> 01:01:36.130
If you wanna go, and so
that's one of the things

01:01:36.130 --> 01:01:39.950
I love about, you know,
where I'm at in my life,

01:01:39.950 --> 01:01:44.118
is that especially in
this town where there's

01:01:44.118 --> 01:01:47.040
no judgment for anybody being anything

01:01:47.040 --> 01:01:48.996
they wanna be and expressing themselves

01:01:48.996 --> 01:01:52.340
because there's so much
of an art centered town.

01:01:52.340 --> 01:01:55.080
Nobody looks at you
funny if you dress weird.

01:01:55.080 --> 01:01:59.710
So it's not that I wanna
stay relevant to the kids,

01:01:59.710 --> 01:02:01.660
it's that I wanna be
able to express myself

01:02:01.660 --> 01:02:04.270
and say, you know, I don't wanna conform,

01:02:04.270 --> 01:02:07.940
I don't wanna conform to
them, 'cause that's kinda sad.

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:10.665
It's like okay, you're in
your 40s, you're trying to...

01:02:10.665 --> 01:02:12.680
If this is how you're trying to connect

01:02:12.680 --> 01:02:15.950
with your 16 and 14 year
old, by dressing like them?

01:02:15.950 --> 01:02:17.940
At any moment you could--

01:02:17.940 --> 01:02:18.773
Crash their party?

01:02:18.773 --> 01:02:20.440
Well, get into some sort of--

01:02:20.440 --> 01:02:21.273
Like a narc.

01:02:21.273 --> 01:02:23.410
Ambiguous athletic event.

01:02:23.410 --> 01:02:25.610
Like maybe there's a uniform
under that tracksuit.

01:02:25.610 --> 01:02:26.443
Right.

01:02:26.443 --> 01:02:28.240
Yeah it aint gonna work for us.

01:02:28.240 --> 01:02:30.934
But tracksuits are classic
though, I will say that.

01:02:30.934 --> 01:02:32.740
Still think we can do tracksuits.

01:02:32.740 --> 01:02:33.573
Find something.

01:02:33.573 --> 01:02:34.430
Yeah you could do that.

01:02:35.300 --> 01:02:36.133
I gave up on that.

01:02:36.133 --> 01:02:39.948
Well you took that, you
wore that tracksuit on GMM.

01:02:39.948 --> 01:02:42.030
It really caused a ruckus.

01:02:42.030 --> 01:02:44.133
I couldn't even wear it the whole episode.

01:02:45.600 --> 01:02:46.433
Couldn't even.

01:02:48.340 --> 01:02:49.440
Well, um--

01:02:49.440 --> 01:02:51.060
But hold on, but I didn't relate it back

01:02:51.060 --> 01:02:52.938
to what we're talking about.

01:02:52.938 --> 01:02:54.606
Relate it and let's shut this down,

01:02:54.606 --> 01:02:55.920
'cause that'd be a dope way to end it.

01:02:55.920 --> 01:02:57.280
Oh there you go, okay.

01:02:57.280 --> 01:02:59.640
I think you've gotta
use it in mixed company

01:02:59.640 --> 01:03:02.420
before it really counts,
so that was a nice try.

01:03:02.420 --> 01:03:04.470
To relate it back to this, and
the question that you asked,

01:03:04.470 --> 01:03:07.357
which is like are we out of touch?

01:03:07.357 --> 01:03:08.770
You haven't picked your word, by the way.

01:03:08.770 --> 01:03:09.623
What's your word?

01:03:11.430 --> 01:03:14.470
What word do you actually
think you can use?

01:03:14.470 --> 01:03:15.303
Facts.

01:03:17.749 --> 01:03:19.509
That could be tough to come with.

01:03:19.509 --> 01:03:21.909
That's a tough one because
it's so definitive.

01:03:22.790 --> 01:03:25.100
It's a closing statement.

01:03:25.100 --> 01:03:28.180
Like when you say facts,
whoever you're talking to

01:03:28.180 --> 01:03:29.751
has nothing to say.

01:03:29.751 --> 01:03:31.673
It's just like oh he just said facts.

01:03:32.640 --> 01:03:33.630
Facts.

01:03:33.630 --> 01:03:36.210
Facts is the end of a thought,

01:03:36.210 --> 01:03:38.445
but dope can just, you
can nestle it in there.

01:03:38.445 --> 01:03:41.440
It's a response though, if
somebody says something facts.

01:03:41.440 --> 01:03:44.740
You know what, I could
do low key or high key.

01:03:44.740 --> 01:03:46.030
Would that be weird?

01:03:46.030 --> 01:03:48.438
Yes but I think that's a good idea.

01:03:48.438 --> 01:03:51.100
Are we back tracking on what we just said?

01:03:51.100 --> 01:03:51.933
Are we...

01:03:53.470 --> 01:03:54.400
That's low key.

01:03:54.400 --> 01:03:56.330
We gotta try it, okay.

01:03:56.330 --> 01:03:58.243
I know that this is inconsistent with what

01:03:58.243 --> 01:04:00.230
I just said about getting on my own lane

01:04:00.230 --> 01:04:02.070
and being an artist and expressing myself.

01:04:02.070 --> 01:04:05.090
I feel like dope is really
easy, it's almost too easy.

01:04:05.090 --> 01:04:07.060
I legitimately like hip-hop, okay?

01:04:07.060 --> 01:04:10.160
I don't like it so that
my son will like me.

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:11.450
So don't judge me.

01:04:11.450 --> 01:04:13.500
Shlitty's a good time.

01:04:13.500 --> 01:04:15.615
I love the rhythms.

01:04:15.615 --> 01:04:17.913
I'm gonna do facts.

01:04:18.810 --> 01:04:19.810
Facts, man.

01:04:19.810 --> 01:04:21.190
All right.

01:04:21.190 --> 01:04:23.430
I'ma be waiting with
baited breath for that one.

01:04:23.430 --> 01:04:26.020
But to relate what I was just
saying about the fashion,

01:04:26.020 --> 01:04:29.690
back to this, is I do think that I am...

01:04:31.970 --> 01:04:34.410
With the fashion thing there's been

01:04:34.410 --> 01:04:36.090
a little bit more effort, right?

01:04:36.090 --> 01:04:38.710
And I do think that some
of it is the fact that

01:04:38.710 --> 01:04:41.217
we're free to dress how we want to,

01:04:41.217 --> 01:04:44.010
no one's gonna fire us
for dressing a certain way

01:04:44.010 --> 01:04:45.822
and there's no dress code.

01:04:45.822 --> 01:04:47.310
Is there a dress code?

01:04:47.310 --> 01:04:49.040
I mean there probably
is a dress code here,

01:04:49.040 --> 01:04:51.880
like you can't wear a
thong and nothing else

01:04:51.880 --> 01:04:53.501
at Mythical Entertainment.

01:04:53.501 --> 01:04:55.980
And if we haven't specified that yet

01:04:55.980 --> 01:04:58.330
we should probably put that
in the employee handbook.

01:04:58.330 --> 01:04:59.163
Wow.

01:05:00.020 --> 01:05:01.760
But you can dress how you want.

01:05:01.760 --> 01:05:05.210
And we've taken full
advantage of that, right?

01:05:05.210 --> 01:05:06.810
Well, we haven't worn a thong.

01:05:07.987 --> 01:05:08.820
Not yet.

01:05:10.750 --> 01:05:12.309
Facts.

01:05:12.309 --> 01:05:13.523
Okay that didn't work.

01:05:14.480 --> 01:05:17.873
But with terminology, lingo,

01:05:19.076 --> 01:05:22.915
we've pretty much continued
to talk the same way.

01:05:22.915 --> 01:05:26.800
Now we've lost our accent
that we had in North Carolina,

01:05:26.800 --> 01:05:29.290
for the most part, it comes
back at certain times.

01:05:29.290 --> 01:05:31.658
But that's really a function
of hearing yourself,

01:05:31.658 --> 01:05:34.390
hours and hours of your
own voice heard back,

01:05:34.390 --> 01:05:36.350
you end up, it happens
with a lot of people,

01:05:36.350 --> 01:05:38.270
your voice, you end up kinda changing--

01:05:38.270 --> 01:05:40.020
What are you getting at?

01:05:40.020 --> 01:05:43.450
I'm just saying that I feel absolutely

01:05:43.450 --> 01:05:47.088
no compulsion to try to use any of this.

01:05:47.088 --> 01:05:49.860
But we just came up with a compulsion,

01:05:49.860 --> 01:05:51.130
that you were gonna do it.

01:05:51.130 --> 01:05:52.910
Yeah we're gonna do it because it's fun.

01:05:52.910 --> 01:05:54.643
We're gonna do it because it's fun,

01:05:54.643 --> 01:05:56.840
and hey, maybe we'll learn something.

01:05:56.840 --> 01:05:57.870
We'll let you know.

01:05:57.870 --> 01:06:01.810
But you let us know,
#EarBiscuits, if you disagree.

01:06:01.810 --> 01:06:06.810
Just feel free to put us
on blast, as they say.

01:06:06.880 --> 01:06:07.850
That's good.

01:06:07.850 --> 01:06:10.510
Rec baby, rec baby, one two three four.

01:06:10.510 --> 01:06:13.230
Rec baby, rec baby, one two.

01:06:13.230 --> 01:06:15.370
You know what I'm gonna do.

01:06:15.370 --> 01:06:17.470
I'm gonna make a recommendation for y'all.

01:06:18.675 --> 01:06:20.240
I'm actually recommending this to you

01:06:20.240 --> 01:06:22.400
'cause I think you'll like it.

01:06:22.400 --> 01:06:26.074
I went into a bookstore because I was told

01:06:26.074 --> 01:06:30.470
that Lost Causes of
Bleak Creek had a placard

01:06:31.948 --> 01:06:34.820
at Vroman's Bookstore in Pasadena

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:36.150
so I went over there
and they didn't have it.

01:06:36.150 --> 01:06:39.516
This was over a month ago now.

01:06:39.516 --> 01:06:42.027
And I was in there, I
was like you know what,

01:06:42.027 --> 01:06:43.843
I'm in a freaking bookstore,

01:06:45.127 --> 01:06:46.814
it's so sad, but I was like--

01:06:46.814 --> 01:06:47.820
Maybe you should buy a book.

01:06:47.820 --> 01:06:48.653
Maybe I should buy a book.

01:06:48.653 --> 01:06:49.770
'Cause I was just thinking I'm just gonna

01:06:49.770 --> 01:06:50.730
look around at all these books,

01:06:50.730 --> 01:06:52.030
but I never thought I'd actually buy one

01:06:52.030 --> 01:06:54.213
'cause I didn't think
I'd actually read one.

01:06:55.050 --> 01:06:57.093
'Cause I'd gotten outta the habit.

01:06:58.970 --> 01:07:01.700
But I picked up a book,
and I will recommend it,

01:07:01.700 --> 01:07:03.780
because I'm really enjoying it.

01:07:03.780 --> 01:07:05.386
The reason why...

01:07:05.386 --> 01:07:07.630
For some reason I went
to the sci-fi section.

01:07:07.630 --> 01:07:09.703
I was like I want a good sci-fi book.

01:07:10.662 --> 01:07:13.954
Got a book called The Three-Body Problem.

01:07:13.954 --> 01:07:16.660
And honestly, one of the
reasons that I got it

01:07:16.660 --> 01:07:21.660
was because it won an
award, it won the Hugo Award

01:07:21.780 --> 01:07:23.667
for best novel in 2015.

01:07:23.667 --> 01:07:26.860
It's part of a trilogy of novels,

01:07:26.860 --> 01:07:29.030
so if I really like it
then I can kinda dig in,

01:07:29.030 --> 01:07:31.417
'cause I really like to dig
into a world if I like it.

01:07:31.417 --> 01:07:34.010
That's why once I started
reading Game of Thrones

01:07:34.010 --> 01:07:36.377
I read all of 'em.

01:07:36.377 --> 01:07:39.429
It's originally written in Chinese

01:07:39.429 --> 01:07:43.728
and translated into
English, which concerned me,

01:07:43.728 --> 01:07:45.540
but it has no impact.

01:07:45.540 --> 01:07:48.320
And then what sealed the
deal and counteracted that

01:07:48.320 --> 01:07:50.790
and made me buy it, not only
did it have a cool cover,

01:07:50.790 --> 01:07:54.239
but Barack Obama endorsed it.

01:07:54.239 --> 01:07:56.294
Wildly imaginative.

01:07:56.294 --> 01:07:58.936
Endorsement's really move you.

01:07:58.936 --> 01:08:03.936
How do you get Barack Obama
to endorse your sci-fi novel?

01:08:03.990 --> 01:08:05.040
He's a sci-fi fan.

01:08:05.040 --> 01:08:06.558
Wildly imaginative.

01:08:06.558 --> 01:08:07.895
That's what he said.

01:08:07.895 --> 01:08:09.303
I was like man, I gotta get this.

01:08:11.230 --> 01:08:14.170
The Three-Body Problem is a
hard science fiction novel.

01:08:14.170 --> 01:08:15.750
Hard science fiction.

01:08:15.750 --> 01:08:17.175
I'm going hard.

01:08:17.175 --> 01:08:18.610
It is the first novel of the

01:08:18.610 --> 01:08:21.010
Remembrance of Earths Passed trilogy

01:08:23.230 --> 01:08:26.090
by writer Liu Cixin.

01:08:26.090 --> 01:08:27.650
That's all I'm gonna say.

